10. Potential New Complaint Group Discovery

The application should identify groups of similar complaints that may represent emerging or previously unseen complaint categories.

This is an important feature.

Do not simply classify every uncertain complaint as "Ambiguous."

Use the existing clustering and similarity-based logic to identify candidate groups.

Requirements

- Analyze complaints requiring review.
- Group semantically similar complaints.
- Use KMeans and the existing embedding approach where appropriate.
- Respect a configurable minimum cluster size.
- Assign candidate group identifiers.
- Leave the candidate group empty for complaints that do not belong to a meaningful cluster.
- Preserve the original result ordering.
- Ensure the number of generated candidate-group values matches the number of input complaints.

Example

Suppose the system identifies these complaints:

- Smart meter display is not working.
- Smart meter screen is blank.
- Smart meter display has stopped functioning.

These complaints may be grouped as a potential candidate group.

Display candidate groups as suggestions requiring human verification.

Important: A candidate group must not automatically become an officially approved complaint category. It is a discovery suggestion, not a confirmed classification.

